<App layout="vertical-sticky" name="Wrapping React components for XMLUI">
  <AppHeader>
    <Text fontWeight="bold">
      Wrapping React components for XMLUI
    </Text>
    <SpaceFiller />
    <Icon
      name="cog"
      tooltip="Inspector"
      width="20px"
      height="20px"
      onClick="inspectorDialog.open()" />
  </AppHeader>

  <NavPanel>
    <NavLink label="Home" to="/" />
    <NavGroup label="Wrapping techniques" initiallyExpanded="{true}">
      <NavLink label="wrapComponent" to="/wrap-component" />
      <NavLink label="wrapCompound" to="/wrap-compound" />
    </NavGroup>
    <NavGroup label="Level 1: Basic wrapping" initiallyExpanded="{true}">
      <NavLink label="Prop forwarding" to="/slider-vs-sliderw" />
      <NavLink label="Free tracing" to="/free-tracing" />
    </NavGroup>
  </NavPanel>

  <Pages>

  <Page url="/">
    <VStack padding="{responsive('0.75rem', '2rem')}" gap="1rem">

      <H1>
        Two levels of component wrapping
      </H1>

      <Markdown>
        <![CDATA[
XMLUI's [`wrapComponent`](/wrap-component) and [`wrapCompound`](/wrap-compound) functions bridge XMLUI markup to React components. They replace the hand-written renderers that have historically been the only way to register a component with the engine.

The hand-written approach requires every prop to be explicitly mapped. If the underlying React component accepts a prop that the renderer doesn't enumerate, that prop is silently dropped. Adding a new prop means editing the renderer. This is fine for a small set of well-known components, but it doesn't scale to third-party libraries or rapid prototyping.

The wrapping approach inverts this: everything forwards by default. Props the metadata doesn't know about still reach the React component via a generic `extractValue()` path. You can start with zero metadata and progressively add it as the component matures.

## Level 1: Basic wrapping

Call [`wrapComponent`](/wrap-component) or [`wrapCompound`](/wrap-compound) with a render component and minimal config. You get three things for free:

**Prop forwarding.** Props that the React component accepts but the XMLUI metadata doesn't declare still flow through. A Radix slider's `inverted` prop, a standard `title` attribute, an `aria-label` for accessibility — they all work without being listed in metadata. Hand-written renderers silently drop anything they don't enumerate. The [Prop forwarding](/slider-vs-sliderw) page demonstrates the difference.

**Automatic tracing.** Both wrappers emit semantic trace events for standard patterns. When a component has a `didChange` event, the wrapper emits `value:change` traces. For `gotFocus`/`lostFocus`, it emits `focus:change`. Hand-written renderers are invisible to the trace system — wrapped components are traceable by default, with no extra code. The [Free tracing](/free-tracing) page shows what this unlocks: accessibility for screenreaders, semantic traces that AIs can reason about, and regression tests generated from user journeys by [trace-tools | target=_blank](https://github.com/xmlui-org/trace-tools).

**Instant gratification.** Minutes of work to wrap a new component. No metadata required upfront — add it incrementally as the component matures: first type annotations for IDE support, then `.md` documentation for the docs site.

## Level 2: Domain-specific trace enrichment

Some components need custom trace logic that goes beyond what generic wrapping provides. FileInput is the example: a generic `value:change` trace for a file upload would log `[object File]` — useless for test generation. The real fix extracts `name`, `size`, and `type` from File objects so the trace says `"photo.jpg"` and trace-tools can generate `setInputFiles()` calls.

Every component with a non-trivial value type potentially needs this level of work. DatePicker values need formatting. ColorPicker values might need hex normalization. Select with objects vs strings. Table row selections. Each one is a small intervention, but across the full component suite it adds up — and it requires understanding each component's semantics, not just mechanical wrapping.

The pages in this app illustrate both levels with working examples.
        ]]>
      </Markdown>

    </VStack>
  </Page>

  <Page url="/wrap-component">
    <WrapComponentPage />
  </Page>

  <Page url="/wrap-compound">
    <WrapCompoundPage />
  </Page>

  <Page url="/slider-vs-sliderw">
    <SliderVsSliderW />
  </Page>

  <Page url="/free-tracing">
    <FreeTracing />
  </Page>

  </Pages>

  <ModalDialog
    id="inspectorDialog"
    title="XMLUI Inspector"
    minWidth="95vw"
    minHeight="95vh">
    <IFrame src="xs-diff.html" width="100%" height="90vh" />
  </ModalDialog>
</App>
